==English==

===Etymology===
"How do you like them apples?” is an expression that supposedly originated during the first World War, when the Allies' anti-tank grenade was colloquially called a “toffee apple” because of its bulb-like appearance on a stick. The phrase was a taunt against the enemy. {{rfv-etym|en}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|En-au-how do you like them bananas.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Phrase===
{{head|en|phrase|head=[[how]] [[do]] [[you]] [[like]] [[them]] [[apple]]s}}?

# {{lb|en|colloquial|rhetorical question}} directed [[jestingly]] or [[mockingly]] at someone who has received [[surprising]] information, [[ridicule|ridiculing]] the situation
#: ''"Our governor has just vetoed a bill that would offer more money to our schools. '''How do you like them apples'''?"''
# {{lb|en|colloquial|rhetorical question|Irish|American}} Used after an actual or proposed action with which the listener might be displeased. Also used after refuting an argument.
#: ''"I can't give you a raise now; if I did, this whole company would go bankrupt, and you wouldn't have a job at all. Now '''how do you like them apples'''?"''

